# Why this file exists
#
# Tailwind CSS v4 builds on a native engine, `@tailwindcss/oxide`. The engine is
# not fetched on its own: it is published as a set of per-platform packages
# (`@tailwindcss/oxide-linux-x64-gnu`, `…-linux-x64-musl`, `…-darwin-arm64`,
# `…-win32-x64-msvc`, …) declared by oxide as *optional* dependencies. npm
# installs the one matching the host OS/CPU and skips the rest.
#
# If anything makes npm omit optional dependencies, the package that disappears
# is the engine itself, and the failure surfaces later from inside oxide as
#
#   Cannot find native binding
#
# which blames the wrong thing. The lockfile is not at fault: `package-lock.json`
# declares every platform package, including the Linux one, with its `os`/`cpu`.
#
# `include=optional` is npm's documented, positive way to keep optional
# dependencies (see `npm help config`: "Use --omit=optional to exclude optional
# dependencies, or --include=optional to include them"). A project `.npmrc`
# outranks a user- or global-level one, so an inherited `omit=optional` on a
# developer machine or a build host can no longer silently disable the native
# engine. `include` is additive — the default set is empty, with everything
# included unless omitted — so prod and dev dependencies are unaffected.
#
# (Do not try to write `omit=` with an empty value: npm rejects it as invalid
# config and ignores it, which silently re-introduces the bug.)
#
# An explicit command-line flag still outranks this file: do not run
# `npm ci --omit=optional` or `npm install --no-optional` in this repository.
# `npm run validate` fails loudly at tests/native-engine.test.ts if the engine is
# ever absent. See docs/workflow.md ("Native engine / optional dependencies").
include=optional
